      "assistant_text": "## Answer\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are both applications of the same architectural principle: **single composition point**.\n\nAccording to [[credential-scoping]], the Hub never holds the Anthropic API key in its own process environment. Instead, a secrets loader reads the key and injects it only into the spawned Ingest or Query agent child processes, immediately before they start. The credential exists only in the short-lived child process that actually uses it \u2014 not in the Hub\u0027s own process environment.\n\n[[runtime-paths]] describes exactly the same pattern for file-system locations: every runtime location the Hub touches (wiki root, data directory, SQLite database, secrets file, agent instruction directories) is resolved in exactly one place at startup. A single path resolver reads the configuration, validates it, and provides the resolved paths downstream. No other part of the codebase discovers locations on its own.\n\n**The connection:** both decisions eliminate scattered decision points in favor of a single authoritative composition. This reduces misconfiguration surface, limits unauthorized access, and makes debugging and auditing easier. When new requirements arrive \u2014 a new agent, a new secret, a new data directory \u2014 the pattern is already clear: extend the single composition point, not scatter the logic across the codebase.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]], since it represents a genuine pattern that neither source page explicitly states on its own.",
